WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:03.033
- [Gary] Anything,
anything I could help with,

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:05.442
or answer any questions,
anything of that nature?

00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:08.101
- [Audience Member] I'm curious to know

00:00:08.101 --> 00:00:10.792
about your social media presence in China.

00:00:10.792 --> 00:00:11.625
- Okay.

00:00:11.625 --> 00:00:13.256
- Because I recently, I was listening

00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:15.373
to one of your talks, and you say

00:00:15.373 --> 00:00:18.147
that Weibo is actually the
fastest-growing channel--

00:00:18.147 --> 00:00:18.980
- Mhm, mhm.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.269
- [Audience Member] at the moment.

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:24.624
Yeah, so just, your experience--

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.874
- Yeah, of course, so I'm--

00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:32.219
I have a presence on Weibo and WeChat,

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:35.112
and, let me see if I can pull it up--

00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:36.195
And, I'm also

00:00:38.117 --> 00:00:39.589
doing quite a bit

00:00:39.589 --> 00:00:41.922
with a Facebook Chinese page

00:00:43.235 --> 00:00:46.818
that is actually growing
ridiculously well.

00:00:48.562 --> 00:00:50.312
And so, in general...

00:00:52.197 --> 00:00:53.114
In general,

00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:55.042
to me,

00:00:55.042 --> 00:00:58.123
I think about this in, kind
of, 30- or 40-year windows,

00:00:58.123 --> 00:01:00.969
and the thought of not having
presence in mainland China

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:05.145
is almost like not existing
in that, kind of, window.

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:08.145
And so, I'm doing KOL collaborations

00:01:09.103 --> 00:01:11.920
with people who are trying to
build a presence in the U.S.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.921
or the rest of the world,

00:01:13.921 --> 00:01:17.183
and obviously, two core
things I speak a lot about,

00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:21.268
entrepreneurship and, I'd
call it family dynamics

00:01:21.268 --> 00:01:25.962
of psychology of what 20-
to 30-year olds go through,

00:01:25.962 --> 00:01:29.015
are clearly over indexing subject matter

00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:33.432
for a lot of mainland Chinese
youthful entrepreneurs,

00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:37.998
and so, it's really, really
growing very rapidly.

00:01:37.998 --> 00:01:41.748
- I was listening to
your YouTube video about

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:47.797
how in your twenties, you
should really just explore

00:01:47.797 --> 00:01:50.214
and not to be to risk-averse,

00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:53.018
and, obviously,

00:01:53.018 --> 00:01:56.061
I've grown up in Finland,
and I've received

00:01:56.061 --> 00:01:59.061
a very sort of traditional Chinese--

00:02:01.697 --> 00:02:02.530
well, not education, but

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.597
the way that I was brought up.
- Point of view.

00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:06.332
- [Audience Member] So,
I mean, that's something

00:02:06.332 --> 00:02:07.513
I don't think that,

00:02:07.513 --> 00:02:11.534
in mainland China, anyone's
ever said to those kids

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:12.642
that, you know--

00:02:12.642 --> 00:02:15.896
- I don't think they've
said it in the U.S. either.

00:02:15.896 --> 00:02:17.075
Or Europe, for that matter.

00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:18.316
And it's interesting, right?

00:02:18.316 --> 00:02:20.059
Like, I think...

00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:21.337
I think about it in two ways,

00:02:21.337 --> 00:02:24.053
I think there's two ways
to win your twenties.

00:02:24.053 --> 00:02:27.067
I think you either go all in and, like,

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:29.369
work your face off and
build that foundation,

00:02:29.369 --> 00:02:32.776
or you go completely
in the other direction,

00:02:32.776 --> 00:02:35.648
and you go higher risk, higher reward,

00:02:35.648 --> 00:02:37.238
to try to just to figure yourself out,

00:02:37.238 --> 00:02:39.624
and taste a lot of different things,

00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:41.957
yeah, I mean, you know, it--

00:02:43.453 --> 00:02:45.656
It actually isn't logical or practical

00:02:45.656 --> 00:02:47.582
the way that we're all brought up

00:02:47.582 --> 00:02:51.146
and like, how we're supposed
to address 22 to 30.

00:02:51.146 --> 00:02:53.852
It's supposed to be,
like, okay, go attack it,

00:02:53.852 --> 00:02:56.509
settle it down and build a foundation.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.357
It just doesn't--

00:02:57.357 --> 00:02:59.566
It, it had a lot more merit

00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:01.867
when people lived to 50 years old.

00:03:01.867 --> 00:03:04.790
It just doesn't have as much merit

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.290
in the way the world is today,

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.036
the opportunities that are gifted.

00:03:09.036 --> 00:03:12.858
Yeah, I mean, a lot of
the things I think about

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:15.183
challenge conventional wisdom,

00:03:15.183 --> 00:03:17.676
because what I think I tend to do is

00:03:17.676 --> 00:03:20.843
layer the reality of the current world

00:03:21.886 --> 00:03:24.472
and not just think what used to work

00:03:24.472 --> 00:03:27.403
in a pre-Internet Industrial Revolution,

00:03:27.403 --> 00:03:29.903
you know, 1960s-70s-80s world.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.367
- [Audience Member] I'm
thinking about tapes for us,

00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:37.433
we have to be renewing
ourselves everyday on social,

00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:40.433
and, ask every other company, too...

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.052
Yeah, but we're doing campaigns,

00:03:45.052 --> 00:03:47.863
we're doing inspirational content,

00:03:47.863 --> 00:03:50.757
so what are the takes like,
from your perspective,

00:03:50.757 --> 00:03:55.757
and, how do you see, like,
commercial campaigns on social?

00:03:56.101 --> 00:03:58.039
- So a thing that I think
will make sense to you guys

00:03:58.039 --> 00:03:59.910
is that, I think that a
company like [redacted]--

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.491
I did a little homework for this meeting,

00:04:03.491 --> 00:04:06.049
I think they get fragmented too much,

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:09.716
meaning, in social, it
is either things like

00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:14.691
Dane &amp; Dale content to fill the pipes,

00:04:14.691 --> 00:04:17.788
which is aspirational and nice, and maybe

00:04:17.788 --> 00:04:20.439
spending too much time
worrying about engagement

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:24.254
and not enough time about
what the business results are.

00:04:24.254 --> 00:04:25.836
Then, they spend a lot of money

00:04:25.836 --> 00:04:29.257
on outdoor and television
and traditional branding--

00:04:29.257 --> 00:04:31.774
To me, the biggest
opportunity in social is

00:04:31.774 --> 00:04:34.625
doing brand work inside of Facebook.

00:04:34.625 --> 00:04:36.154
To me, the number one thing I would do

00:04:36.154 --> 00:04:37.871
if I took over marketing for [redacted],

00:04:37.871 --> 00:04:41.731
is I would produce 50-second
to four-minute videos

00:04:41.731 --> 00:04:46.152
for Facebook with the
intent of brand and sales.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:48.030
- That long videos?

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:48.996
- Long videos.
- Really?

00:04:48.996 --> 00:04:49.829
- Mhm.

00:04:49.829 --> 00:04:51.813
- [Audience Member] What
is the reason for that?

00:04:51.813 --> 00:04:52.974
- Because they work.

00:04:52.974 --> 00:04:55.141
- [Audience Member] We have seen that

00:04:55.141 --> 00:04:57.394
shorter videos seem to work--

00:04:57.394 --> 00:04:58.526
- Shorter videos work for

00:04:58.526 --> 00:05:01.140
the vanity metrics of social media,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.307
and the way you judge
shareability or consumption.

00:05:07.616 --> 00:05:11.138
But when you look at actual
attribution to a transaction

00:05:11.138 --> 00:05:12.319
or the ability to--

00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:13.499
Let's play it out.

00:05:13.499 --> 00:05:17.061
Okay, so you make a
20-second video on Facebook,

00:05:17.061 --> 00:05:19.894
let's say it gets 100,000 engaged.

00:05:21.579 --> 00:05:23.605
Liked, shared, commented.

00:05:23.605 --> 00:05:27.708
And then you run a 2:19 video
that has a lot more breath

00:05:27.708 --> 00:05:29.449
to, like, what you're about.

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:33.511
That one may have 17,000 people watch it;

00:05:33.511 --> 00:05:35.855
it may cost you more money on media to get

00:05:35.855 --> 00:05:38.673
17,000 engaged than 100,000.

00:05:38.673 --> 00:05:42.113
But, when you go to re-target those 17,000

00:05:42.113 --> 00:05:43.407
versus the 100,000,

00:05:43.407 --> 00:05:48.251
the math tends to work out
on the smaller, deeper group

00:05:48.251 --> 00:05:50.751
than the wider, shorter group.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.037
And so, I think that, being in [Redacted],

00:05:55.037 --> 00:05:57.014
unlike being a bottled water company,

00:05:57.014 --> 00:05:58.511
is when they go to re-target,

00:05:58.511 --> 00:06:01.561
there's still a retailer and
they can't see all the math.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.468
For you, when you re-target,
it's black and white.

00:06:04.468 --> 00:06:06.652
You'll be able to see the CAC and the LTV

00:06:06.652 --> 00:06:08.830
on the transaction that
you're looking for.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:09.854
So I think the biggest mistake

00:06:09.854 --> 00:06:10.692
a lot of the big companies make

00:06:10.692 --> 00:06:13.113
is they do social media for
the sake of social media,

00:06:13.113 --> 00:06:15.784
not for the sake of the actual
business skills they have.

00:06:15.784 --> 00:06:19.454
And they don't hold
traditional dollars they spend

00:06:19.454 --> 00:06:21.357
to the level of accountability

00:06:21.357 --> 00:06:23.247
that they do in the new world,

00:06:23.247 --> 00:06:26.128
because the new world
has so much more metrics.

00:06:26.128 --> 00:06:28.970
You have no idea if there's
three-second or ten-second

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.689
viewability on your commercials,

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:33.779
you don't know the
impressions of your outdoor,

00:06:33.779 --> 00:06:36.503
you've "accepted" it because
it's always been done,

00:06:36.503 --> 00:06:38.933
but you hold the new stuff
to a level of scrutiny

00:06:38.933 --> 00:06:40.383
that you don't with the old stuff.

00:06:40.383 --> 00:06:41.216
- Yeah.

00:06:42.094 --> 00:06:45.893
- So, to me, that's where
the big miss is, for...

00:06:45.893 --> 00:06:50.119
- [Audience Member] How do
you see, like, very technical,

00:06:50.119 --> 00:06:52.951
advertisement in social media.

00:06:52.951 --> 00:06:56.286
Should those be done or
should it be just for brand--

00:06:56.286 --> 00:07:00.388
- I think both work. I think
branding and selling work.

00:07:00.388 --> 00:07:03.733
There's enormous amounts of
selling to be done on Facebook;

00:07:03.733 --> 00:07:05.844
I think you can't be half-pregnant.

00:07:05.844 --> 00:07:07.457
I think a lot of people try

00:07:07.457 --> 00:07:11.323
to hide sales into
branding, and vise-versa,

00:07:11.323 --> 00:07:13.989
I think there's a lack
of a good conversation

00:07:13.989 --> 00:07:15.568
between the difference
between a piece of content

00:07:15.568 --> 00:07:18.592
that's meant for branding and
one that's meant for sales,

00:07:18.592 --> 00:07:21.839
and I think most organizations over index,

00:07:21.839 --> 00:07:24.422
in respecting one or the other.

00:07:25.383 --> 00:07:27.364
You have some companies
that don't like social media

00:07:27.364 --> 00:07:30.830
because all they want to
do is be DR marketers,

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.744
and that's why they favor Google,

00:07:32.744 --> 00:07:35.271
and then you have other people
who don't value social media

00:07:35.271 --> 00:07:37.052
because all they want to do is brand work,

00:07:37.052 --> 00:07:40.011
and they think television or sponsorships

00:07:40.011 --> 00:07:41.584
are "better brand work."

00:07:41.584 --> 00:07:44.114
And that's why social,
and specifically Facebook,

00:07:44.114 --> 00:07:45.948
has this incredible opportunity right now,

00:07:45.948 --> 00:07:47.479
'cause its underpriced attention,

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:49.660
because not enough money's going into it

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.355
either from DR or brand.

00:07:52.355 --> 00:07:53.821
Yet, if you look at Wish,

00:07:53.821 --> 00:07:56.134
if you look at so many other companies,

00:07:56.134 --> 00:07:58.391
they do their marketing
and their selling--

00:07:58.391 --> 00:08:01.217
Purple Mattress, many
other companies, Lola,

00:08:01.217 --> 00:08:03.391
in that environment--

00:08:03.391 --> 00:08:05.350
That's why I'm so bullish on it.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.742
The issue is, like anything,

00:08:07.742 --> 00:08:11.876
the ROI of these two markers
is completely predicated

00:08:11.876 --> 00:08:14.890
on my talent to draw
with these two markers.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:18.341
And I think that there's a sophistication,

00:08:18.341 --> 00:08:20.103
both mathematically and artistically

00:08:20.103 --> 00:08:21.899
and strategically and psychologically

00:08:21.899 --> 00:08:26.899
that it takes to be good at
any marketing in any medium,

00:08:26.901 --> 00:08:30.112
and I think most people
are applying old principles

00:08:30.112 --> 00:08:31.582
into this new environment,

00:08:31.582 --> 00:08:33.950
which is why they're
not getting the results.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.833
To your point, this bottled
water company would rather

00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:39.746
put something like, you know,

00:08:39.746 --> 00:08:42.329
a #WednesdayWisdom post because

00:08:43.773 --> 00:08:47.098
they're gonna get 87 likes
on, you know, the post

00:08:47.098 --> 00:08:48.303
without really understanding

00:08:48.303 --> 00:08:51.720
if that's going to lead to selling, here.

00:08:53.058 --> 00:08:55.371
I think there's a lack of thoughtfulness

00:08:55.371 --> 00:08:57.018
in most people's--

00:08:57.018 --> 00:08:57.951
you know--

00:08:57.951 --> 00:09:01.406
overall marketing
strategy, digital strategy,

00:09:01.406 --> 00:09:02.967
and then social strategy.

00:09:02.967 --> 00:09:05.313
- [Audience Member] When you
were looking our Facebook site,

00:09:05.313 --> 00:09:06.286
what did you think?

00:09:06.286 --> 00:09:07.239
What were our--

00:09:07.239 --> 00:09:08.293
- What I couldn't tell--

00:09:08.293 --> 00:09:09.778
What I was looking for, more, was

00:09:09.778 --> 00:09:12.320
how much marketing you
were doing actually,

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:13.913
in the traditional world.

00:09:13.913 --> 00:09:14.987
When I was looking at Facebook,

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:16.376
the biggest thing I don't know is

00:09:16.376 --> 00:09:18.550
are you running Facebook
ads that I don't see

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:19.491
on your feed? You know...z

00:09:19.491 --> 00:09:21.241
- Which I would hope you
are, and more importantly,

00:09:21.241 --> 00:09:23.393
is far more important
than what I would see.

00:09:23.393 --> 00:09:25.555
I was less analyzing your social,

00:09:25.555 --> 00:09:28.000
I was more trying to get a sense of

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.945
how much traditional media you were doing

00:09:31.945 --> 00:09:34.551
in the name of branding.

00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:37.017
Because, as I've just said earlier,

00:09:37.017 --> 00:09:38.820
the number one thing I
would do if I were you

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.020
is much more branding inside of Facebook.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:42.853
That, to me is, you know, I would argue,

00:09:42.853 --> 00:09:44.853
the unique point of view

00:09:48.325 --> 00:09:50.275
by comparison to most right now,

00:09:50.275 --> 00:09:53.572
that I think has the most upside.

00:09:53.572 --> 00:09:56.470
- [Audience Member] What
do you think about bots

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:59.792
and artificial intelligence,
now that it's like--

00:09:59.792 --> 00:10:02.588
and we know that many of us at [Redacted]

00:10:02.588 --> 00:10:06.059
have been taking those
bots very seriously,

00:10:06.059 --> 00:10:08.550
they have been doing good
work inside Messenger

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.383
and so on...

00:10:09.383 --> 00:10:10.478
We aren't there yet,

00:10:10.478 --> 00:10:12.759
we have a bot qualification--

00:10:12.759 --> 00:10:15.610
- You need to get there.
- It's taking baby steps.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:16.674
- You need to get--

00:10:16.674 --> 00:10:19.294
You are gonna get caught
if you fall behind too much

00:10:19.294 --> 00:10:21.695
because all of your customer service

00:10:21.695 --> 00:10:23.783
is gonna happen through that environment.

00:10:23.783 --> 00:10:24.649
You're gonna save a fortune.

00:10:24.649 --> 00:10:25.482
- Yeah?

00:10:25.482 --> 00:10:27.454
- It's gonna be better, more efficient,

00:10:27.454 --> 00:10:30.832
higher conversion, and lower cost.

00:10:30.832 --> 00:10:32.478
You need to be serious about Messenger,

00:10:32.478 --> 00:10:34.746
and I think, when you
look at mainland China,

00:10:34.746 --> 00:10:36.663
the U.S., VK in Russia,

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.271
the AI message bot infrastructure

00:10:40.271 --> 00:10:41.916
is going to be very important.

00:10:41.916 --> 00:10:44.785
Consumers will interact with those bots

00:10:44.785 --> 00:10:46.055
because they're gonna get--

00:10:46.055 --> 00:10:47.041
it's time saving.

00:10:47.041 --> 00:10:48.204
- [Audience Member] Yeah, that is true.

00:10:48.204 --> 00:10:49.769
- It's gonna take time...

00:10:49.769 --> 00:10:50.785
It's gonna take two or three years

00:10:50.785 --> 00:10:52.529
for it to be the behavior that I think

00:10:52.529 --> 00:10:54.353
a lot of technologists are looking for,

00:10:54.353 --> 00:10:58.134
but, I mean, I'm a very big fan of it.

00:10:58.134 --> 00:10:59.370
It will be there.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.149
- [Audience Member] How
do you see, like, social,

00:11:01.149 --> 00:11:04.162
should it be in-house
or should it be, out--

00:11:04.162 --> 00:11:06.726
- I think everything should be in-house.

00:11:06.726 --> 00:11:09.363
Which is funny, considering
what I do for a living.

00:11:09.363 --> 00:11:10.730
I think, what I've learned is

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:12.154
that most companies aren't--

00:11:12.154 --> 00:11:14.715
the reason they don't do
in-house is two reasons--

00:11:14.715 --> 00:11:16.680
One, strictly from a Wall Street-

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.674
or publicly-traded company standpoint,

00:11:18.674 --> 00:11:22.025
they don't want the expenses on their PnL,

00:11:22.025 --> 00:11:23.979
which hurts them in public markets,

00:11:23.979 --> 00:11:26.695
or two, they just don't retain the talent,

00:11:26.695 --> 00:11:28.588
and they feel like we're--

00:11:28.588 --> 00:11:31.283
you know, they lose that continuity.

00:11:31.283 --> 00:11:34.063
To me, anything you can do in-house

00:11:34.063 --> 00:11:36.278
is always going to be interesting

00:11:36.278 --> 00:11:40.028
unless they're somebody
who's hungry enough...

00:11:40.028 --> 00:11:41.113
The way I would run--

00:11:41.113 --> 00:11:43.675
if I owned a company, the
way I would play agencies

00:11:43.675 --> 00:11:47.165
is I would work with independent shops

00:11:47.165 --> 00:11:50.028
who are on their way to try
to sell to holding companies,

00:11:50.028 --> 00:11:52.711
'cause they're gonna care more,

00:11:52.711 --> 00:11:53.703
they're just different.

00:11:53.703 --> 00:11:56.125
So, I think the biggest
issues with agencies

00:11:56.125 --> 00:12:00.227
and with brands is agencies
are publicly-held companies,

00:12:00.227 --> 00:12:02.501
you know, so much consolidation, that--

00:12:02.501 --> 00:12:04.473
- Yeah, and they don't
love our brand like...

00:12:04.473 --> 00:12:07.076
We, kind of see in that window--

00:12:07.076 --> 00:12:08.413
- I think that's right.

00:12:08.413 --> 00:12:11.111
But I also think what agencies do do well,

00:12:11.111 --> 00:12:14.674
including others is, they're
able to see your brand

00:12:14.674 --> 00:12:15.507
in a different way than you are.

00:12:15.507 --> 00:12:16.340
- [Audience Member] Yeah?

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.457
- Everybody thinks their
kids are the prettiest,

00:12:18.457 --> 00:12:21.152
so I think that's where
they can bring sensibility.

00:12:21.152 --> 00:12:22.633
My biggest problem with agencies is

00:12:22.633 --> 00:12:24.879
they don't produce enough work

00:12:24.879 --> 00:12:26.879
for the cost associated.

00:12:27.871 --> 00:12:30.640
I think that's where we're
really kind of winning,

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:31.473
is we're--

00:12:31.473 --> 00:12:34.705
First of all, I built the
agency mainly to buy brands.

00:12:34.705 --> 00:12:37.873
So, I have far more ambition
to become my clients

00:12:37.873 --> 00:12:40.266
than to be an agency,

00:12:40.266 --> 00:12:42.544
which aligns our interests better

00:12:42.544 --> 00:12:45.890
and what we realized after
we got great at media was,

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.192
oh my God we have to
produce so much content

00:12:48.192 --> 00:12:51.726
to take advantage of the media landscape,

00:12:51.726 --> 00:12:55.854
so we built our own 50,000
square foot production facility,

00:12:55.854 --> 00:12:59.883
and had a much deeper religion
on the cost of creative

00:12:59.883 --> 00:13:02.321
that I think the market
has been accustomed to,

00:13:02.321 --> 00:13:06.539
without giving up this
magical idea of quality.

00:13:06.539 --> 00:13:08.944
You know, when I first
got into the business,

00:13:08.944 --> 00:13:11.723
I came from startup land
and entrepreneur land

00:13:11.723 --> 00:13:12.939
and Silicon Valley,

00:13:12.939 --> 00:13:17.156
and I would look at a video,
you know a commercial,

00:13:17.156 --> 00:13:18.531
and I literally was like,

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:21.561
"How the hell does this cost $600,000?"

00:13:21.561 --> 00:13:23.995
You know, I just couldn't get there.

00:13:23.995 --> 00:13:25.988
So I think, our great ambition--

00:13:25.988 --> 00:13:26.821
Back to the--

00:13:26.821 --> 00:13:28.142
So our motto is,

00:13:28.142 --> 00:13:31.367
if you're a brand that does a commercial

00:13:31.367 --> 00:13:33.716
and it costs $800,000 in out-of-pocket

00:13:33.716 --> 00:13:35.795
and creative and production,

00:13:35.795 --> 00:13:37.479
we try to convince that client to do

00:13:37.479 --> 00:13:40.343
six or five or four or three

00:13:40.343 --> 00:13:43.687
long-form Facebook videos
for that same cost,

00:13:43.687 --> 00:13:48.465
which can, first, fit
different demographics, right?

00:13:48.465 --> 00:13:50.942
Because when you do one
commercial, it's just vanilla,

00:13:50.942 --> 00:13:51.951
whereas, you know,

00:13:51.951 --> 00:13:55.915
if you star an Asian, you
know, family in their twenties

00:13:55.915 --> 00:13:59.129
and target Asian Americans or
Europeans in their twenties,

00:13:59.129 --> 00:14:02.168
you're gonna over index in relevance,

00:14:02.168 --> 00:14:05.516
and then we also think the
media costs of distribution

00:14:05.516 --> 00:14:07.638
are more efficient,

00:14:07.638 --> 00:14:09.175
because, I think the biggest conversation

00:14:09.175 --> 00:14:11.095
that you also probably want
to have on the media front,

00:14:11.095 --> 00:14:15.261
is there's potential reach
and then there's actual reach.

00:14:15.261 --> 00:14:19.147
And I think the market trades a potential

00:14:19.147 --> 00:14:21.912
and doesn't layer common sense.

00:14:21.912 --> 00:14:25.762
I mean, there's almost no
market left in the world

00:14:25.762 --> 00:14:29.485
that OTT hasn't eaten up
such a substantial percentage

00:14:29.485 --> 00:14:32.590
of television content consumption,

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.954
that it's made every commercial
reared grossly overpriced.

00:14:36.954 --> 00:14:39.103
- [Audience Member] At
least hearing [Redacted]

00:14:39.103 --> 00:14:41.214
influence your landscape,

00:14:41.214 --> 00:14:44.339
we're seeing the same faces everywhere

00:14:44.339 --> 00:14:45.936
- Well it's a small market.

00:14:45.936 --> 00:14:47.868
- [Audience Member] Endorsing the--

00:14:47.868 --> 00:14:49.096
- Yeah, over and over.

00:14:49.096 --> 00:14:50.025
- [Audience Member] Everything.

00:14:50.025 --> 00:14:52.081
- Yeah, I think, to your point,

00:14:52.081 --> 00:14:54.078
one thing I did look at, and,

00:14:54.078 --> 00:14:59.078
I had a meetup last night
with four of the ten faces...

00:14:59.121 --> 00:15:01.587
Yeah, I think there's a
lot to be left desired

00:15:01.587 --> 00:15:03.236
in influencer marketing.

00:15:03.236 --> 00:15:05.732
But I think that's where
you probably want to look at

00:15:05.732 --> 00:15:07.887
the global nature of your business,

00:15:07.887 --> 00:15:10.363
and look at the pockets
where you can play--

00:15:10.363 --> 00:15:14.756
Or, using those same
faces in other markets.

00:15:14.756 --> 00:15:16.491
So I do think they're inexpensive.

00:15:16.491 --> 00:15:19.939
So I do think, what's
interesting about your ten faces,

00:15:19.939 --> 00:15:23.119
is in a U.S.-centric
campaign, with that, kind of,

00:15:23.119 --> 00:15:25.563
level of YouTube, which is really

00:15:25.563 --> 00:15:28.040
what we're seeing [Redacted] following,

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:29.675
there probably is an inexpensive way

00:15:29.675 --> 00:15:32.034
to do some very clever collaborations

00:15:32.034 --> 00:15:35.001
where you might be able
to take those four to ten,

00:15:35.001 --> 00:15:39.152
do some sort of collab with
two or three U.S.-centric--

00:15:39.152 --> 00:15:40.744
in the U.S., there's
something that I think

00:15:40.744 --> 00:15:42.245
would really work for you, which is

00:15:42.245 --> 00:15:44.578
family vlogging is very big,

00:15:45.453 --> 00:15:48.427
and some of it can get pretty affluent.

00:15:48.427 --> 00:15:51.565
And I could see a lot of
interesting collabs of, like,

00:15:51.565 --> 00:15:54.436
that family going to [Redacted].

00:15:54.436 --> 00:15:56.753
There's something to be done
that I think you could hack.

00:15:56.753 --> 00:15:59.715
So I think, if you're
thinking for this market,

00:15:59.715 --> 00:16:02.563
your domestic market, I would say

00:16:02.563 --> 00:16:05.949
how underpriced Facebook
and Instagram ads are,

00:16:05.949 --> 00:16:08.702
is probably the strategy I would deploy.

00:16:08.702 --> 00:16:10.994
If I thought about the influencer market,

00:16:10.994 --> 00:16:14.635
I would use them as
ambassadors on the outside,

00:16:14.635 --> 00:16:16.359
because what's great
about YouTube is this,

00:16:16.359 --> 00:16:17.568
let me play it out for you.

00:16:17.568 --> 00:16:21.680
You take a YouTuber with
600,000 subscribers,

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.545
he's still cheap.

00:16:23.545 --> 00:16:26.549
Like you could probably
give a couple [Redacted],

00:16:26.549 --> 00:16:28.923
or, fine, a little bit of bucks,

00:16:28.923 --> 00:16:31.008
but in the ecosystem of YouTube,

00:16:31.008 --> 00:16:34.976
if there's people with a
million subscribers in the U.S.,

00:16:34.976 --> 00:16:37.981
the idea to do a collaboration
with him is exciting

00:16:37.981 --> 00:16:40.772
'cause they wanna siphon fans from him.

00:16:40.772 --> 00:16:44.017
So he's more valuable
as an asset out there,

00:16:44.017 --> 00:16:45.155
you see where I'm going?

00:16:45.155 --> 00:16:47.405
And this is what I was talking
about with thoughtfulness,

00:16:47.405 --> 00:16:49.198
I think the level of sophistication and

00:16:49.198 --> 00:16:51.930
the way people fragment all
these different things--

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:53.444
and that's why, I think, we've invested

00:16:53.444 --> 00:16:54.708
so much in strategy--

00:16:54.708 --> 00:16:57.523
our biggest growth has been--

00:16:57.523 --> 00:16:59.312
so we used to meet somebody like you,

00:16:59.312 --> 00:17:00.248
and if we wanted to do business,

00:17:00.248 --> 00:17:02.020
we're like, okay, you can
give us your media budget,

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:03.464
you can give us your creative budget,

00:17:03.464 --> 00:17:04.908
and I think the thing that we've realized

00:17:04.908 --> 00:17:05.989
in the last couple of years is,

00:17:05.989 --> 00:17:08.572
or for €150,000 strategy thing,

00:17:09.586 --> 00:17:12.044
we can save clients millions of dollars

00:17:12.044 --> 00:17:14.599
with their creative and media partners,

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:16.932
and it's a good way to
get the value out of us.

00:17:16.932 --> 00:17:18.920
So, I think even how we're talking now,

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.266
that would have been how we probably

00:17:20.266 --> 00:17:22.118
would have analyzed your
influencer strategy,

00:17:22.118 --> 00:17:25.416
go use their following
count to do collaborations

00:17:25.416 --> 00:17:27.377
in other markets with bigger influencers

00:17:27.377 --> 00:17:28.799
who are trying to siphon

00:17:28.799 --> 00:17:30.302
and use them as a gateway,

00:17:30.302 --> 00:17:33.666
as an ambassador to your brand
through that collaboration.

00:17:33.666 --> 00:17:37.333
To me, it's unemotional
assets versus costs.

00:17:39.115 --> 00:17:39.948
Right?

00:17:39.948 --> 00:17:41.894
So, for me, I'm not in love
with Facebook or Instagram,

00:17:41.894 --> 00:17:44.016
I'm in love with the price of their ads.

00:17:44.016 --> 00:17:46.599
(upbeat music)

